<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muş</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mush</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muş, also spelled Mush, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, capital of Muş Province, on a fertile plain west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>. It was the centre of the historic Armenian district of Taron, held by the Mamikonian and later Bagratid princes, with the great monastery of Surb Karapet nearby. Its Armenian population was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mus.jpg" alt="Muş" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mush_1213.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Muş is one of the principal cities of the predominantly Kurdish-inhabited region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. It is the seat of Muş Province and Muş District. The town and the surrounding plain are often covered in fog in the mornings, and the name is sometimes linked to the Armenian word mshush, meaning fog. The 17th-century traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> tells of a giant mouse, created by Nimrod, that destroyed the city, which was then called Muş, the Persian word for mouse. Others connect the name with the Mushki or the Mysians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The date of foundation is unknown, but a settlement is believed to have existed by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menua">Menua</a>, king of Urartu, around 800 BCE, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription was found near the city. An inscription of the Urartian king Argisti I, who reigned from 785 to 765 BCE, was found near Kepenek Castle. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city was the centre of the Taron region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and is first mentioned as a city in Armenian manuscripts of the 9th and 10th centuries.</p><p>Taron came under the Bagratid dynasty in the late 8th century and was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 969. After the Seljuk victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 it passed to the Ahlatshahs, and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Kara Koyunlu</a>. Between the 10th and 13th centuries Muş grew into a major city of an estimated 20 to 25 thousand people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> apparently took it without a battle in 1387. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> held it before the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> took the town and region in the early 16th century, and it kept a large Armenian population under Ottoman rule.</p><p>At the turn of the twentieth century the city had around 20,000 inhabitants, of which 11,000 were Muslims and 9,000 were Christian Armenians. In June and July 1915, during the Armenian genocide, over 140,000 Armenians of the Muş sanjak, in 234 villages and towns, were targeted; the city's Armenians were killed only after the surrounding villages had been destroyed. Russian forces captured the town in February 1916, and the Second Army under Mustafa Kemal Pasha retook it in 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipality had about 120,699 inhabitants in 2022, a majority of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Hidden Armenians among them. Sights include the Murat Bridge, Muş Castle, Kepenek Castle and mosques showing Seljuk influence, among them the Ulu Mosque of the 14th century, once an Armenian church. Medieval Armenian monasteries and churches near the city, such as Arakelots and Surb Karapet, are mostly ruins; Arakelots was dynamited by local officials in the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muş” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1366 Shaykh Uways Jalayir defeated their leader Birdi Khwaja at Mosul and his brother Bayram Khwaja at the battle of <b>Mush</b>, and from about 1375 the Qara Qoyunlu were Jalayirid vassals ruling from Mosul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1890 the London paper The Era described the melody as written over a hundred years before, and it appears to have been adapted from the folk song &quot;<b>Mush</b>, Mush&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/botany-bay">Botany Bay</a></li><li><blockquote>Taron, the plain around <b>Mush</b>, had long been a Mamikonian land.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-taron">Principality of Taron</a></li></ul></section>
